# Styling a workbook

A workbook renders in a browser, so it needs design — type, color, spacing,
layout — and we have been quietly leaning on it the whole time: the dashboard's
board, the [[Score chart]] island, every page you have read. This file makes
styling first-class and, like everything else here, keeps it in **Elixir**. There
is no separate CSS toolchain — no Node, no PostCSS, no Tailwind binary to install.
You declare a **theme** and use **utility classes**; the weave compiles them to CSS
in-sandbox, the same lane that compiles your Rust and your Elixir.

It is Tailwind's ergonomics with Elixir's source. You get the familiar, fast
utility classes — `p-4`, `bg-mint`, `grid`, `gap-3` — but the scale, the palette,
and the breakpoints are an Elixir value you own, not a `tailwind.config.js` written
in another language you have to keep in sync.

## The theme is a value

Design tokens are just data. The palette, the spacing scale, the type ramp, the
radii — a plain struct whose field types fall out of the defaults, exactly like
[[Lead]]. One theme is the single source of truth for the whole workbook.

defmodule Theme do
  defstruct palette: %{
              paper: "#f7f6f1",
              ink:   "#16171a",
              mint:  "#aee5c2",
              sky:   "#a8d4f0",
              peach: "#f3c5a3",
              muted: "#6b6a63"
            },
            space:  [0, 4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32, 48, 64],
            radius: %{sm: 6, md: 10, lg: 16, pill: 999},
            type:   %{family: "Geist, system-ui, sans-serif", base: 15, ratio: 1.2}
end

theme :sales, %Theme{}

The `theme :sales` declaration registers it. The weave reads the tokens and emits
two things: a set of CSS custom properties (`--paper`, `--mint`, `--space-4`…) so
islands and hand-written CSS share the exact same values, and the utility classes
below, named from the scale.

## Utilities, generated from the theme

You style markup the utility way — small, composable classes — but each class is
generated from a token, so it can never drift from the theme. `bg-mint` is exactly
`background: var(--mint)`; `p-4` is `padding: var(--space-4)` (16px on this scale);
`text-ink`, `radius-md`, `grid`, `gap-3` likewise. Here is the dashboard's board
card, styled with nothing but utilities:

<article class="card grid gap-3 p-5 bg-paper text-ink radius-md">
  <h3 class="text-lg">Initech</h3>
  <div class="row gap-2 text-muted">score 88 · scored</div>
</article>

Because the classes come from the theme, rebranding is a one-line edit to `Theme` —
every `bg-paper` and `p-4` across every page moves together. And because the weave
knows which classes a bundle actually uses, it ships only those: the stylesheet in
`salesapp.html` is tree-shaken to the utilities the workbook really references, with
no purge step to configure.

## Three layers, one set of tokens

Styling lands in three places, and the theme tokens flow through all of them so
nothing has to be redefined:

- **Pages and server-rendered regions** use the utility classes directly in their
  markup, as above. This is most of the app.

- **Client islands** ([[Score chart]], [[Trend sparkline]]) keep their own scoped
  `<style>` for component internals — a Svelte or Solid component owns its private
  CSS — but they read the shared tokens through the `--mint` / `--space-*` variables,
  so an island matches the page without copying a single hex code.

- **One-off component styles** can be written as plain Elixir that returns scoped
  CSS, co-located with the unit it styles, the same way prose co-locates with code:

server compute :badge do
  def style(:hot),  do: css(bg: :peach, color: :ink, radius: :pill, px: 3)
  def style(:cold), do: css(bg: :sky,   color: :ink, radius: :pill, px: 3)
end

`css/1` resolves its keyword options against the active `theme`, so `bg: :peach`
becomes `background: var(--peach)` and `px: 3` becomes horizontal padding from the
space scale. Same tokens, whether you write a utility class, an island `<style>`,
or an Elixir `css` call.

## Why styling is Elixir too

The pattern is the one this whole workbook runs on. You author in Elixir; the
WebAssembly fell out of the modules, the WIT fell out of [[The Contract]], and now
the **CSS falls out of your theme**. There is one design system, owned as a value,
shared by pages, islands, and components alike — and the weave turns it into a
single tree-shaken stylesheet with no toolchain to install and nothing to keep in
sync. Comprehensive styling, and still just Elixir.
